<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/perimeter-area-volume">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Mathematics, on the foundational track. It assumes
    no prior geometry — we start from three errands you already understand: fencing
    a garden, carpeting a floor, and filling a fish tank. Every new word is
    explained the moment it appears.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>choose the right measure — perimeter, area, or volume — for a real question;</li>
    <li>use the workhorse formulas for rectangles, triangles, parallelograms and trapeziums;</li>
    <li>handle circles: circumference, area, arcs and sectors;</li>
    <li>compute surface areas and volumes of prisms, cylinders, cones and spheres;</li>
    <li>break a compound shape into pieces, and predict how all three measures scale.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Start with three errands</h2>
  <p>
    You want to fence a garden. You want to carpet a floor. You want to fill a
    fish tank. Three errands, three different kinds of "how big is it?" — and
    each one has its own measure. <strong>Perimeter</strong> is the distance
    around the edge: a length, measured in metres, the kind of bigness a fence
    cares about. <strong>Area</strong> is the amount of surface inside, measured
    in square metres — the kind of bigness a carpet or a coat of paint cares
    about. <strong>Volume</strong> is the amount of space filled, measured in
    cubic metres (or litres, where 1 L = 1,000 cm³) — the kind of bigness a water
    tank or a lung cares about.
  </p>
  <p>
    The units are not decoration; they are the logic. A square metre literally is
    a square one metre on a side, so a 4 m × 3 m floor holds twelve of them —
    which is why area comes out as a <em>product</em> of lengths. Every area
    formula you will meet is a clever way of counting unit squares without laying
    them by hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the deeper story is how the three measures behave when things change
    size. Double a cube's side and its <strong>perimeter doubles</strong>, its
    <strong>area quadruples</strong>, its <strong>volume octuples</strong>. That
    mismatch — length ×1, area ×4, volume ×8 — is the <em>square–cube law</em>,
    and it quietly runs biology, engineering, cooking and the price of
    everything. First the formulas, then the law that falls out of them.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="drag the side and watch three measures drift apart — volume always wins" widget="sliderLab" config={scaleCfg} />

  <h2>Perimeter and area: the workhorse formulas</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Perimeter</strong> is honest addition: walk the boundary and add the
    sides. For a rectangle of length l and width w, that is P = 2(l + w).
    <strong>Area</strong> of the same rectangle is A = l × w — the unit-square
    counting argument above. A square of side a has area a², which is the very
    reason squaring is called squaring.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>triangle</strong> is half a rectangle in disguise: cut any
    triangle's bounding rectangle along the apex and the two leftover pieces
    rearrange, giving A = ½ × base × height. The height means perpendicular
    height, not the slanted side — the classic exam trap. A
    <strong>parallelogram</strong> shears into a rectangle of the same base and
    height (A = bh). A <strong>trapezium</strong> with parallel sides a and b is
    the average of the two parallel sides times the height, A = ½(a + b)h,
    because it is the average of two parallelogram-like slices. Every one of
    these is provable by cutting and rearranging — which is why they are worth
    knowing <em>cold</em>: they are not conventions but theorems about counting
    squares.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example: a triangular garden bed has base 6 m and perpendicular height
    2.5 m — area ½ × 6 × 2.5 = 7.5 m². Fencing it costs per metre of
    <em>perimeter</em>; mulching it costs per square metre of <em>area</em>. Two
    different questions, two different measures, two different prices —
    conflating them is how DIY budgets die.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> perimeter walks the edge, area counts unit squares,
    and each formula is a cutting-and-rearranging argument you could re-derive.
  </p>

  <h2>Circles, arcs and sectors: the special number π</h2>
  <p>
    Every circle shares one magic ratio: the circumference divided by the
    diameter is the same for <em>all</em> circles, everywhere. That number is π ≈
    3.14159, an irrational number whose digits never repeat or end. So
    <strong>C = 2πr</strong> (or πd), and the area is <strong>A = πr²</strong>.
    You can see the area formula by slicing a disc into thin wedges and standing
    them in a row: the row's length is half the circumference (πr) and its height
    is the radius, so the "rectangle" has area πr × r.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Arcs and sectors</strong> are fractions of the whole. A sector — a
    pie slice — with angle θ° is θ/360 of the circle, so its arc is (θ/360) × 2πr
    and its area is (θ/360) × πr². The fraction is the whole trick: pie slices
    are shares.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real-world case: a 30 cm pizza cut into 8 has sector angle 45°, arc length
    (45/360) × π × 15 ≈ 5.9 cm of crust, and area (45/360) × π × 15² ≈ 88 cm².
    And the classic decision: is one 40 cm pizza more pizza than two 30 cm ones?
    One 40 cm holds π × 20² ≈ 1,257 cm², while two 30 cm hold 2 × π × 15² ≈
    1,414 cm² — the smaller pair wins, because area grows with the square of the
    radius, not the diameter. Do the arithmetic every time; pizza intuition is
    notoriously bad.
  </p>

  <h2>Surface area and volume: nets of 3-D solids</h2>
  <p>
    In three dimensions, <strong>volume</strong> counts unit cubes and
    <strong>surface area</strong> is the total area of the boundary. The best way
    to picture surface area is to unfold the solid into a flat <em>net</em>: a
    cereal box is six rectangles, so its surface area is their sum, and its
    volume is length × width × height. A <strong>prism</strong> — any shape
    extruded along a length — has V = (area of cross-section) × length. A
    cylinder is a circular prism, <strong>V = πr²h</strong>, with surface area
    2πr² + 2πrh (two discs plus a rolled rectangle of height h and width 2πr —
    check the net).
  </p>
  <p>
    The pointed cousins: a <strong>cone</strong> holds V = ⅓πr²h — exactly a
    third of the cylinder it sits in, as slicing arguments prove — and a
    <strong>sphere</strong> has A = 4πr², V = (4/3)πr³. Notice what the sphere's
    surface formula implies: the sphere's surface is exactly four of its
    great-circle shadows, a result Archimedes considered his greatest discovery
    and asked to have carved on his tomb.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "surface area and volume grow together." They do not — and
    the gap between them is one of the most consequential ratios in science.
  </p>

  <h2>Compound shapes: build and subtract</h2>
  <p>
    Real shapes are compound: an L-shaped room, a running track, a window with a
    semicircular top. The universal strategy is <strong>decompose and
    combine</strong> — split the shape into rectangles, triangles and sectors
    whose formulas you know, then add the areas. For holes and cut-outs, compute
    the big shape and <strong>subtract</strong>. A running track is a rectangle
    flanked by two semicircles (together one full circle): a 100 m straight and
    36.5 m radius bends give area 100 × 73 + π × 36.5² ≈ 7,300 + 4,186 ≈ 11,486
    m². The same logic in reverse gives <strong>parts of shapes</strong>:
    knowing the whole and one part, the other follows.
  </p>
  <p>
    This add-and-subtract habit is also unit-keeping in miniature. Track which
    measure (m, m², m³) each term belongs to, and most errors catch themselves —
    because you can never add a length to an area.
  </p>
  <p>
    A pool run, end to end: a rectangular pool 10 m long, 4 m wide, with a 1.2 m
    shallow end sloping to a 1.8 m deep end has average depth 1.5 m, so volume =
    10 × 4 × 1.5 = 60 m³ = 60,000 litres. Filling it is billed by the cubic
    metre; the liner is quoted in square metres — about 82 of them (40 of floor
    plus 42 of sloping wall); the safety fence is sold by the perimeter,
    2 × (10 + 4) = 28 m. Three measures, three prices, one hole in the ground:
    measurement literacy in a single purchase.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unit conversion hides the square–cube law inside it: converting
    <em>lengths</em> multiplies by the linear factor, but areas by its
    <em>square</em> and volumes by its <em>cube</em>. 1 m = 100 cm, so
    1 m² = 100² = 10,000 cm² — a hundred times more than the naive answer — and
    1 m³ = 100³ = one million cm³ (which is exactly why 1 litre = 1,000 cm³ and
    a cubic metre holds 1,000 litres).
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked compound example, top to bottom: a window is a 1.2 m × 0.9 m
    rectangle topped by a semicircle of diameter 0.9 m (radius 0.45). Rectangle:
    1.08 m². Semicircle: ½ × π × 0.45² ≈ 0.318 m². Total glazing ≈ 1.40 m². Its
    perimeter is two sides (1.2 + 0.9) plus the semicircular arc (π × 0.45 ≈
    1.41) ≈ 3.51 m — and note the frame needs the <em>perimeter</em> while the
    glass quote asks for the <em>area</em>: two prices, two measures, one shape.
    A worked subtraction: a 5 m × 4 m lawn with a 2 m × 1 m pond needs
    (20 − 2) = 18 m² of turf — compute the whole, remove the part, keep the
    units squared throughout.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a cube twice as tall holds twice as much." Doubling
    the side multiplies volume by 2³ = 8 and area by 2² = 4 — a 20 cm cube is not two
    10 cm cubes but eight of them (with only four times the surface). The error is
    harmless with dice and ruinous with concrete orders, which is why quotations are
    always given in volume, never in "sizes".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> compound shapes are add-and-subtract puzzles over
    formulas you already own — and every conversion or comparison should be
    checked against its units.
  </p>

  <h2>The square–cube law: where the formulas collide</h2>
  <p>
    Now drag the slider with intent. Perimeter scales as a, area as a², volume
    as a³ — so as anything grows, volume races ahead of surface. The ratio
    readout, volume per unit of surface, grows linearly with size, and that
    single line explains a surprising amount of the world.
    <strong>Strength scales with area, weight with volume:</strong> a mouse
    falls off a table and bounces; a horse falls and breaks bones — Galileo
    worked this out in 1638, and it is why elephants have pillar legs while ants
    lift fifty times their weight. <strong>Heat loss scales with surface, heat
    storage with volume:</strong> big animals stay warm and small ones must eat
    almost continuously to hold temperature, which is why populations of the
    same species tend larger in cold climates. <strong>Cells are small</strong>
    because diffusion through a surface cannot feed a volume that grows too
    fast, and chips have cooling problems for the same reason. Even recipes do
    not scale linearly: doubling a dish doubles the volume, but the pan area —
    where browning happens — grows less, so cooking times stretch.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a 1:10 scale model of a car weighs about a tenth of
    the car." It weighs about a thousandth: every length shrinks by 10, every area by
    100, and the volume — which is what fills with the model's material — by 1,000. This
    is why scale models float and fly so differently from the real thing, and why wind-tunnel
    and ship-tank engineers must correct their forces rather than trust the model raw.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A rectangular room is 5 m long and 4 m wide. How much skirting board (perimeter) and how much carpet (area) does it need?</li>
    <li>Which is more pizza: one 36 cm pizza, or two 24 cm pizzas?</li>
    <li>A water tank is a cylinder of radius 1 m and height 2 m. How many litres does it hold?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) Perimeter = 2 × (5 + 4) = 18 m of skirting;
    area = 5 × 4 = 20 m² of carpet — two measures, two purchases. (2) One 36 cm
    pizza: π × 18² ≈ 1,018 cm². Two 24 cm pizzas: 2 × π × 12² ≈ 904 cm². The
    single large pizza wins, because area grows with the square of the radius.
    (3) Volume = πr²h = π × 1² × 2 ≈ 6.28 m³, and each cubic metre is 1,000
    litres — about 6,280 litres.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The deep structure behind "length, area, volume" is <strong>dimensional analysis</strong>:
    every physical quantity carries units, and formulas are only valid when the dimensions
    balance. Perimeter formulas are linear in length; area formulas quadratic; volume cubic.
    Test any claimed formula by its dimensions and half of all errors evaporate — if someone
    proposes area = 2πr for a disc, the units alone (metres, not metres²) refute it, no
    measurement needed. The technique, formalised in the 19th century, extends far beyond
    geometry: it is how engineers check equations and how physicists derive scaling laws
    (terminal velocity ∝ √(mass/drag area), for instance) without solving anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two final refinements. First, π is <strong>irrational</strong> (proven by Lambert, 1761)
    and <strong>transcendental</strong> (Lindemann, 1882) — no fraction of integers, and no
    root of any integer polynomial. The transcendence closes an ancient question: since
    constructible numbers are algebraic, squaring the circle with compass and straightedge is
    impossible, full stop. Second, for irregular shapes the exact formulas give way to
    integration — the area under a curve, the volume of a solid of revolution — and to
    numerical estimation: count the squares a shape covers, or use <strong>Cavalieri's
    principle</strong> (solids with equal cross-sectional areas at every height have equal
    volumes), which is precisely why the cone is ⅓ of its cylinder and why the hemisphere
    trick works. Geometry's formulas, it turns out, are just the closed-form answers to
    "how many unit squares?" — and when the answer has no closed form, the counting
    goes on.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Galileo, Discourses (1638), Two New Sciences; Archimedes, On the Sphere and Cylinder; OpenStax, Prealgebra (measurement units); Cambridge IGCSE Mathematics syllabus (E5).</p>
